### 0.1 Start rule (unchanged from V4.0)

```
IF npm run phase15:audit != complete-frozen:
    Phase 1.6 implementation = BLOCKED
    Planning / research / specification = ALLOWED

IF npm run phase15:audit == complete-frozen:
    Freeze Phase 1.5 evidence and SHA
    Begin Phase 1.6 PR sequence
```

### 0.2 What changed in V5

- Closed the eval-baseline discrepancy between V4's table and live audit (verified,
  not assumed).
- Folded Addendum V1 in fully: freshness discipline is now the default rule for
  every continuity object in this document, not a bolt-on.
- Added the Capacity Watcher as a named subsystem (Section 2.2, Section 29A).
- Two real staleness incidents (observation file, `.ai/handoff.md`) are cited
  directly as the evidence justifying the above, per operating law 6.

### 0.3 What changed in V6

- Added Section 29B: a rule-based **task classifier** and **task-class → provider
  table** that decides which provider a WorkUnit *should* use, extending the
  existing capacity-triggered Claude↔Codex handoff (Section 29A) with a second,
  independent routing dimension — task fit, evaluated every time, not only at
  failure.
- Extended the provider adapter layer to include **free-tier candidates** (Gemini
  CLI, Aider, OpenHands) behind the same `ProviderAdapter` interface used by
  Claude/Codex, gated by role, risk tier, and trust tier — not by capacity alone.
- Added a **trust-tier table (T0–T4)** for vetting any third-party agent/tool before
  it is eligible for routing.
- Per operating law 3 and the honest-scoping precedent already set in Section
  29A.4: **none of Section 29B is validated against real Beacon workloads.** It is
  recorded as a routing hypothesis with a required eval path, not a default
  behavior change. See Section 29B.7.

### 0.4 What changed in V6.1

- **Correction, not addition:** Gemini CLI is removed as the T1 free-tier
  candidate throughout Section 29B. Dated evidence (Section 29B.4, Appendix C)
  shows its free tier ended 2026-06-18, five days after V6.0 was authored — this
  is exactly the kind of stale-snapshot failure Section 29A.1 already warns
  against, now happening to this document's own content rather than to Beacon's
  runtime state.
- Free-tier candidates are re-architected as **two independently swappable
  pieces** — a harness (the CLI tool) and a backend (the model API) — rather
  than one bundled vendor tool. See new Section 29B.10.
- Added Section 29B.11: the routing decision is formalized as **two separate
  decision moments** — an initial per-prompt routing decision (classify → filter
  → rank → select) and a distinct mid-execution switch decision (poll → threshold
  → continue or switch) — previously collapsed into a single "provider router"
  step.
- Added open decision 29B.9.5: whether mid-execution capacity switching (Section
  29A.3's mechanism) should extend to free-tier backends, or whether free-tier
  exhaustion should simply fail the task back to the user given its lower-stakes
  role assignment. Not resolved in this revision.

### 0.5 What changed in V6.2

- Added Section 17A: the **prompt-to-learning-journey pipeline** — every captured
  thought that resolves to a concept worth explaining is routed toward an
  interactive **explorable explanation** (the established HCI term for
  manipulable, progressive-disclosure learning content, per Appendix B's new
  citations) rather than defaulting to static prose.
- Added Section 17B: an **evaluation checkpoint** for auto-generated learning
  content, modeled on the EE-Eval framework (Appendix B) — an explorable
  explanation's interaction quality is checked before it is trusted, not assumed
  from successful rendering alone.
- Added Section 19A: a **formal ADR supersession contract** — `Proposed →
  Accepted → Deprecated / Superseded by ADR-NNNN`, accepted ADRs treated as
  immutable, matching established industry practice (Appendix B). A
  `LearningPackage` now versions alongside the ADR it explains: when a decision
  is superseded, its learning content is marked historical and preserved, not
  deleted or silently overwritten — directly satisfying the "history preserver"
  requirement this addendum was built around.
- Added Section 24A: the **three-site deployment model** — Section 24's existing
  private/formal/public projections formalized as three actually-deployed
  properties (business, decisions, learning) reading from one canonical lineage,
  not three separately-authored copies.
- Updated Section 15 (Visual Understanding Router): explorable-first is now the
  stated default for any concept extracted from a decision; static text is the
  fallback, not the baseline.
- Updated Appendix A with the `LearningPackage` contract and supersession fields
  on `ADRRef`; updated Section 25's ERD accordingly.
- Per operating law 3: **none of Addendum V4 is validated against a real Beacon
  learning package.** It is a routing/pipeline hypothesis with a required eval
  path (Section 17B), consistent with how Addenda V2 and V3 were scoped.

### 0.6 What changed in V6.3

- Added Section 24B: **deployment mechanics for the three sites** — one monorepo,
  one canonical content layer, three build-time projection filters, three deploy
  targets. Resolves the specific open item flagged at the end of V6.2: whether the
  three sites should be one repo with three build targets or three separately
  maintained repositories. This document takes the former, as the direct
  infrastructure-layer application of the "one lineage, multiple projections"
  principle Section 3 already states for the data layer.
- Each site's build-time filter reuses an existing enforcement point rather than
  adding new ones: the business build reuses the existing `prepublish.mjs` gate;
  the decisions build reuses Section 19's ADR authority boundary (only
  non-`proposed` ADRs are ever built); the learning build reuses Section 17B's
  `trusted`/`historical` status gating.
- Updated Section 31's PR-8 exit evidence to include a same-build-produces-all-
  three-sites-correctly check.

### 0.7 What changed in V6.4

- **Correction:** Section 29B.3's implementation row previously named Aider+Groq
  and Aider+Cerebras as generic free-tier candidates defaulting behind paid
  providers. Dated benchmark and model-catalog research (2026-08-13, Appendix C)
  showed two problems with this: the paid-first default didn't match the actual
  intended strategy, and the named free model (Llama 3.3 70B) was the *weakest*
  option Groq/Cerebras host, not a reasonable default.
- Added Section 29B.3.1: **locked vs. open-ranked roles.** Architecture stays
  locked to Claude/Codex regardless of health or cost — the stakes of an
  architecture mistake justify spending paid capacity there specifically.
  Implementation becomes genuinely open-ranked: paid and free candidates compete
  by fit, cost, and health, with no paid-first default.
- Corrected the free-tier model recommendation to gpt-oss-120b, GLM-4.7, and
  Qwen3 235B — the actual strongest models Groq and Cerebras host as of
  2026-08-13 — replacing the Llama-3.3-only assumption.
- Added Section 29B.10.4: **dual-homing for free-tier backends** — where the
  identical model is served by two providers (gpt-oss-120b on both Cerebras and
  Groq), prefer that pairing for primary/fallback over two different models on
  two different providers, since a same-weights failover doesn't change output
  format or behavior mid-task.
- Added a **benchmark methodology caveat** to Section 29B.7: SWE-bench Verified
  is saturated and contamination-flagged as of 2026 and should not be used to
  differentiate frontier models; SWE-bench Pro scores are only comparable within
  the same harness (vendor-self-reported vs. Scale's standardized SEAL run can
  differ by 10-30 points on the identical model). No benchmark number should
  enter this document without stating which harness produced it.
- Added open decision 29B.9.7: free-tier model **catalogs**, not just free-tier
  **availability**, need periodic re-verification — evidence showed Cerebras's
  hosted-model list changing substantially within months.

### 0.8 What changed in V6.5

No new subsystem. Eight hardening additions, all sourced from concepts already
present but not yet centralized or machine-checkable:

- **Section 1, new operating law:** the epistemic-state distinction — KNOWN ≠
  BELIEVED ≠ PROPOSED ≠ AUTHORIZED ≠ EXECUTED ≠ OBSERVED — stated as the single
  sentence closest to Beacon's defining idea.
- **Section 1A (new):** twelve numbered Architectural Invariants (INV-001
  through INV-012), machine-testable, collecting rules that were previously
  scattered as prose across many sections.
- **Section 1B (new):** the Frozen vs. Experimental Architecture Registry —
  explicit status per major component, so a future revision cannot accidentally
  promote a hypothesis to settled fact by omission.
- **Section 8A (new):** Source Identity and Content Hashing — `contentHash` added
  to the Source contract, because a URL can change content without changing
  address; "what exactly did Beacon see" must be preserved.
- **Section 25A (new):** Lifecycle State Machines for ResearchThread,
  UnderstandingVersion, DecisionCandidate, ADR, and LearningPackage — explicit
  transition contracts, not prose descriptions.
- **Section 26A (new):** Event and Replay Semantics — event identity, idempotency,
  causation, correlation, and a replay requirement, closing the "what happens on
  duplicate or out-of-order events" gap.
- **Section 27A (new):** a Data Classification Matrix, connecting the existing
  trust-tier model (29B.4) directly to what data each tier may be exposed to,
  rather than leaving that connection implicit.
- **Section 29B.1, new principle:** provider routing is an optimization layer,
  never a source-of-truth layer — the router decides *who executes*; it must
  never influence *what Beacon believes, remembers, decided, or is authorized to
  execute*.
- **Section 29B.6, resolved:** the free-tier mid-execution failure policy (open
  decision 29B.9.5, unresolved since V6.1) — same-model/compatible fallback
  before pausing, with architecture, release, and riskTier 2-3 implementation
  remaining paid-only regardless.
- **Section 33A (new):** the Beacon Evaluation Suite — a Beacon-specific
  benchmark (ARCH, RESEARCH, UNDERSTANDING, CODE, REVIEW, LEARNING categories),
  replacing "which model scores highest on a generic leaderboard" with "which
  provider performs best on Beacon's actual workload," consistent with the
  benchmark methodology caveat added in V6.4.
- **Section 33B (new):** a Learning Evaluation Protocol — concept → objective →
  baseline vs. explorable comparison → prediction/retrieval/transfer → measured
  outcome, replacing "the page looked good" with a testable claim, extending
  rather than replacing Section 17B's qualitative checklist.
- **Section 31, PR sequence extended:** PR-0A (invariants + state machines),
  PR-0B (event/replay semantics), PR-3A (Beacon Evaluation Suite), PR-5B
  (Learning Evaluation), inserted so evaluation grows alongside the architecture
  instead of arriving only after full build-out.

**Explicit non-goal, stated in the source review and preserved here:** V6.5 adds
no new infrastructure. Section 30's restraint list (no graph database, no full
RAG, no dedicated capacity-monitoring service until evidence justifies them)
stands unchanged.

### 0.9 What changed in V6.5.1

A second external review scored V6.5 and found it not yet safe to implement
unchanged. Four real, verified corrections — not new material:

1. **ADR lifecycle contradiction, fixed.** Section 25A's state diagram was
   missing the DEPRECATED transition that Section 19A's prose and Appendix A's
   `ADRRef.status` enum both already specified. All three now agree:
   `PROPOSED → ACCEPTED → {DEPRECATED, SUPERSEDED}`.
2. **LearningPackage lifecycle contradiction, fixed.** Appendix A's
   `LearningPackage.status` enum was missing `evaluating`, which Section 25A's
   state diagram and Section 17B's checkpoint both already assumed exists. The
   enum now reads `draft | evaluating | trusted | historical`.
3. **PR-0A's premature invariant tests, fixed.** V6.5 scheduled automated tests
   for all twelve invariants in PR-0A, before five of their owning entities
   (Claims, UnderstandingVersion, DecisionPackage, public projections, outcome
   feedback) exist. PR-0A now tests only the seven invariants checkable from
   contracts alone; the remaining five activate in the PR that introduces their
   owning capability — Section 31's table now states explicitly which PR that is
   for every invariant, and states the rule generally: **the PR that introduces
   the capability also activates its invariant.**
4. **Capacity Watcher's build-sequence position, corrected.** It sat on the main
   critical path (PR-0.5) despite Section 29A.4 already stating no real capacity
   event has ever demonstrated the need. New Section 29A.7 resolves this: the
   Capacity Watcher's *design* is unchanged, but it moves to an evidence-gated
   side track off the main PR sequence. Section 29A.7 also records a genuinely
   new, verified finding — Claude Code's `/usage` command and `oauth/usage`
   endpoint do expose structured five-hour/seven-day utilization percentages
   (Appendix C) — which resolves the *technical feasibility* half of this
   question without resolving the separate *priority* question, and the section
   is explicit that those are not the same question.

**Structural adoption, not correction:** Section 1B's registry was restructured
from one status column to three independent axes (Architecture, Implementation,
Validation), following the review's finding that collapsing all three into one
word made true statements ("Three-site projection: FROZEN") read as if they
implied something false ("...and therefore built"), when architecture and
implementation status are genuinely independent facts.

**What was not adopted in this pass**, left as improvement suggestions rather
than corrections: a fully deterministic, machine-checked `FreshnessPolicy`
(Section 8A/9 remain principle-level); an explicit evidence-threshold gate before
any future new subsystem is proposed (Section 1B's rule already requires
evidence for promotion, but doesn't yet formalize a threshold). Both are
reasonable next candidates, not contradictions requiring immediate correction.

