## 1. Executive thesis and operating laws

**PRODUCT THESIS.** Beacon Phase 1.6 is not another wiki. It is the system that turns
natural human thought into durable, evidence-grounded understanding, visual mental
models, decision-ready knowledge and traceable authority — without making any AI
provider the source of truth.

> **THE DEFINING DISTINCTION *(new in V6.5)*.** Beacon must distinguish what it
> knows, what it believes, what it has decided, what it is authorized to execute,
> and what it has merely hypothesized:
>
> `KNOWN ≠ BELIEVED ≠ PROPOSED ≠ AUTHORIZED ≠ EXECUTED ≠ OBSERVED`
>
> Every entity in the canonical data model (Section 25) occupies exactly one of
> these states at a time, and no process in this document is permitted to treat
> one as another — a `Claim` (believed) is not a `DecisionCandidate` (proposed);
> a `DecisionCandidate` is not an accepted `ADR` (authorized); an accepted ADR is
> not a completed `WorkUnit` (executed); a completed WorkUnit is not yet
> production evidence (observed) until outcomes are actually captured (Section
> 21).

- Terminal-first: the normal working interface is `beacon`; browser surfaces are
  projections for learning, inspection and review.
- Research is claim-oriented, not article-oriented.
- Understanding is versioned. New evidence creates a new `UnderstandingVersion`
  rather than silently overwriting history.
- Visuals are compiled representations chosen by knowledge shape and learning
  intent, not decoration quotas.
- Human acceptance is the authority threshold.
- Phase 1.6 ends at an accepted ADR plus a compact Decision Package. Frozen Phase 1.5
  remains the execution constitution.
- Production evidence flows back into the knowledge system.
- **Continuity state (provider capacity, gate status, deployment state) is ephemeral
  by construction and must carry its own observation timestamp — nothing in this
  system treats a snapshot as durable past its freshness window.** *(new in V5)*

### 1.1 Operating laws

1. Providers own computation; Beacon owns memory, policy, evidence and continuity.
2. Knowledge proposes; ADRs authorize; the Chief of Staff orchestrates; agents
   execute; CI verifies; production provides evidence.
3. Unknown remains unknown. Do not manufacture confidence, understanding scores,
   evidence, external state, **or capacity/session-limit incidents that did not
   occur.** *(clarified in V5, per the real precedent this project already set)*
4. Every important claim must have provenance, status, boundary conditions and a
   path to re-evaluation.
5. Every important decision must be traceable backward to evidence and forward to
   implementation/outcomes.
6. Use the minimum sufficient infrastructure and add heavier systems only when
   measured evidence justifies them. **(Addendum V1's Capacity Watcher and freshness
   rules are justified this way — by two real incidents, not speculation.)**

---

## 1A. Architectural Invariants *(new in V6.5)*

These collect rules already stated in prose across earlier sections into one
numbered, machine-testable list. An invariant here is not new policy — each one
cites where it already existed. Centralizing them exists so implementation can
write one test per invariant rather than reconstructing the rule from scattered
paragraphs.

| ID | Invariant | Existing source |
|---|---|---|
| INV-001 | Provider context MUST NOT become canonical Beacon memory. | Section 6, Section 29 |
| INV-002 | Only an accepted ADR may authorize Phase 1.5 execution. | Section 19's authority invariant |
| INV-003 | An accepted ADR MUST NOT be mutated. Superseding decisions create a new ADR. | Section 19A |
| INV-004 | Every research-derived claim MUST have traceable evidence provenance. | Section 10, Section 21 |
| INV-005 | `UnderstandingVersion` N MUST remain addressable after N+1 exists. | Section 11 |
| INV-006 | Ephemeral continuation state MUST be revalidated before authorization use. | Section 29A.2's binding revalidation rule |
| INV-007 | Provider eligibility MUST be evaluated against data classification before provider selection. | Section 29B.5 step 2; formalized in Section 27A |
| INV-008 | An untrusted adapter MUST NOT receive capabilities above its trust tier. | Section 29B.4 |
| INV-009 | Public projections MUST NOT bypass canonical privacy/authority gates. | Section 24, Section 27 |
| INV-010 | Free-tier or experimental providers MUST NOT possess release authority. | Section 29B.3 (release: locked, paid-only) |
| INV-011 | Phase 1.5 MUST receive a bounded `DecisionPackage`, not the full research transcript. | Section 20 |
| INV-012 | Production outcomes MUST remain capable of generating new understanding. | Section 4 step 12, Section 21 |

> **HOW THESE ARE USED.** An invariant is violated the moment a described
> behavior would break it — this table is not aspirational language, it is a
> checklist an implementation PR must be checked against before merge (Section
> 33's acceptance strategy should grow one test per invariant over time).

---

## 1B. Frozen vs. Experimental Architecture Registry *(new in V6.5, restructured in V6.5.1)*

The single biggest risk at this point in the project is not a missing idea — it
is an unvalidated idea quietly being treated as settled. This registry exists to
prevent that. **Frozen** means: do not reopen without new evidence forcing it.
**Experimental** means: still a hypothesis, still gated by its own eval path,
even though it is fully specified.

**V6.5.1 correction:** a single status column conflated *design maturity* with
*implementation maturity* — the Three-Site Deployment Model, for example, was
listed as FROZEN, which is true of the architecture decision, but its actual
build doesn't land until PR-8 (Section 31), and nothing about that row said so.
The registry now tracks three independent axes, so a component can truthfully be
architecturally settled and simultaneously unbuilt and unverified — that
combination is not a contradiction, and hiding it inside one column made it look
like one.

| Component | Architecture | Implementation | Validation | Where specified |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Canonical lineage (Prompt → ... → Outcome) | **FROZEN** | PARTIAL | SIMULATED | Section 4, Section 25 |
| Human ADR authority boundary | **FROZEN** | PARTIAL | SIMULATED | Section 19 |
| Provider-neutral memory | **FROZEN** | PARTIAL | SIMULATED | Section 6, Section 29, INV-001 |
| ADR supersession model | **FROZEN** | NOT STARTED | UNTESTED | Section 19A, PR-6A |
| Three-site projection from one lineage | **FROZEN** | NOT STARTED | UNTESTED | Section 24A, Section 24B, PR-8 |
| Freshness discipline | **FROZEN** | PARTIAL | REAL — two real staleness incidents, Section 29A.1 | Section 29A |
| Provider trust-tier model (the principle) | **FROZEN PRINCIPLE** | NOT STARTED | UNTESTED | Section 29B.4 |
| Task classification | **EXPERIMENTAL** | NOT STARTED | UNTESTED | Section 29B.2 — log-only until eval evidence, per 29B.7 |
| Free-tier routing | **EXPERIMENTAL** | NOT STARTED | UNTESTED | Section 29B.3 — no adapter yet run against a real WorkUnit |
| Capacity prediction | **EXPERIMENTAL** | NOT STARTED | UNTESTED | Section 29A — deprioritized off the critical path in 29A.7 pending evidence |
| Explorable-first learning | **EXPERIMENTAL** | NOT STARTED | UNTESTED | Section 17A — no `LearningPackage` validated against a real learner |
| LearningPackage evaluation | **EXPERIMENTAL** | NOT STARTED | UNTESTED | Section 17B, Section 33B |
| Specific free models named (gpt-oss-120b, GLM-4.7, Qwen3 235B) | CURRENT OBSERVATION | n/a | n/a | Section 29B.3 — correct as of 2026-08-13, re-verification cadence undecided (29B.9.7) |
| Provider benchmark rankings cited anywhere in this document | CURRENT OBSERVATION | n/a | n/a | Section 29B.7's methodology caveat applies to all of them |

> **WORKED EXAMPLE, from the source review.** Capacity Watcher reads as:
> **Architecture: EXPERIMENTAL. Implementation: NOT STARTED. Validation:
> UNTESTED.** All three true simultaneously, all three independently informative
> — much harder for an agent or a human to compress into a single misleading
> word.

> **RULE.** A component's *Architecture* axis may only move from EXPERIMENTAL to
> FROZEN by passing the eval path already specified for it (Section 33's
> acceptance strategy, Section 33A, Section 33B) — never by a future revision
> simply asserting confidence. *Implementation* only advances by an actual PR
> landing (Section 31). *Validation* only advances by SIMULATED (fixture, not
> live) or REAL (actual incident or actual eval run) evidence — never by
> assertion. CURRENT OBSERVATION entries expire on their own terms
> (re-verification, not promotion) — they either get re-confirmed or get
> corrected, the way Addendum V3 corrected the Gemini CLI entry and Addendum V5
> corrected the free-model entry.

