## 2. Inherited Phase 1.5 execution constitution

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-execution-constitution.mmd" title="Inherited Phase 1.5 execution constitution" /%}
*Figure 2. Phase 1.6 creates knowledge and authority packages; frozen Phase 1.5 performs controlled execution.*

### 2.1 Capabilities inherited unchanged

| Inherited capability | Phase 1.6 rule |
|---|---|
| Eight agent roles | Reuse the fixed role set; do not create a parallel research execution workforce. |
| Context compiler | Extend inputs/references where needed; do not rebuild provider-specific prompt assembly. |
| Claude/Codex provider boundary | Reuse provider-neutral routing, capacity status and continuation state. |
| Token auditor and budgets | Use existing budget controls for Phase 1.6 work packages. **Exception (V5): any inherited mechanism reading a cached/static file for gate, capacity, or deployment state must comply with the revalidation rule in Section 29A before Phase 1.6 relies on it — tracked as Phase 1.5 closure-hardening, not new Phase 1.6 scope.** |
| Tool policy / risk / council | Research does not bypass pre-tool or review authority. |
| Publication readiness | All Phase 1.6 PRs inherit localReady/publicationReady/external evidence discipline. |
| CI / attestation / promotion / rollback | No alternate Phase 1.6 delivery pipeline. |

### 2.2 What Phase 1.6 owns

- Thought capture and knowledge classification
- Research Threads and Question Trees
- Source/Evidence/Claim lineage
- UnderstandingVersions and Understanding Packages
- Visual/interactive learning compilation
- Decision Readiness and Decision Candidates
- ADR request preparation and architecture/documentation impact calculation
- Knowledge/outcome feedback and public/private projections
- **Capacity Watcher: predictive provider-usage monitoring and automatic handoff
  triggering** *(new in V5 — see Section 29A)*
- **Task Classifier and task-class provider table: rule-based, evidence-gated
  routing of a WorkUnit to the provider best suited to its task class, independent
  of capacity** *(new in V6 — see Section 29B; planning-stage, not yet authorized
  for live routing control)*

---

## 3. Product surfaces and authority

| Surface | Audience | Authority | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `/lab` | Private founder + agents | Exploratory/current understanding | Questions, research, evidence, models, contradictions, active learning, readiness. |
| `/decisions` | Engineering/governance | Formal accepted decision | ADR authority and consequences. |
| `/docs` | Engineering/reference | Canonical implementation/project memory | Current architecture, contracts, runbooks, operational truth. |
| `/building` | Public curated | Narrative only | Sanitized story of what was learned, decided, built and observed. |
| `beacon` CLI | Founder/engineering | Control plane, not policy authority | Capture thought, search knowledge, continue work, inspect state, **see live provider-capacity status** — without browser switching. |

> **ONE LINEAGE, MULTIPLE PROJECTIONS.** Do not create four manually synchronized
> knowledge bases. *(See Section 24A for these formalized as three actually-deployed
> sites — business, decisions, learning — added in V6.2.)*

---

## 4. Thought-to-knowledge lifecycle

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-thought-to-knowledge-lifecycle.mmd" title="Thought-to-knowledge lifecycle" /%}
*Figure 3. Complete thought → research → understanding → authority → execution → learning loop.*

1. Capture each submitted terminal thought as a private-by-default `PromptObserved` event.
2. Classify it as operational, continuation, existing knowledge, new research
   question or changed understanding.
3. Attach a `ProjectContextSnapshot` before external research.
4. Decompose broad questions into a Question Tree with explicit unknowns and
   decision relevance.
5. Collect evidence through explicit lanes; normalize rather than store only
   summaries.
6. Create or update Claim Ledger entries, including contradiction and boundary
   conditions.
7. Compile `UnderstandingVersion` N and an Understanding Package.
8. Use the Visual Understanding Router and active checks only where they improve
   comprehension.
9. When Decision Readiness is satisfied, generate a Decision Candidate for human
   disposition.
10. Only human acceptance can create `ADRRequested`; only accepted ADRs cross the
    execution authority line.
11. Link WorkUnits, PRs, artifacts, deployments and outcomes back to the originating
    thread.
12. Create `UnderstandingVersion` N+1 when production evidence changes the mental
    model.

---

## 5. Event-sourced lineage and immutable history

The durable unit is a lineage of typed events and canonical objects, not an AI chat
transcript.

- Append-only history for thought, research, claims, understanding and decision
  transitions.
- No silent mutation of earlier beliefs. Superseding creates a new version with
  explicit links.
- Events carry actor, time, visibility, project-context reference and provenance
  metadata.
- Event payloads are bounded; large source material is referenced, not copied.
- Sensitive prompt/response bodies are private and not persisted unless explicitly
  justified.

```
PromptObserved
  -> ThoughtClassified
  -> ResearchThreadCreated / ResearchThreadUpdated
  -> EvidenceAdded / ClaimCreated / ClaimStatusChanged
  -> UnderstandingVersionCreated
  -> DecisionCandidateCreated
  -> DecisionAccepted
  -> ADRRequested / ADRAccepted
  -> DecisionPackageCreated
  -> WorkUnitCreated ... OutcomeObserved
  -> RetrospectiveCreated
  -> UnderstandingVersionCreated
```

---

## 6. Four-layer memory architecture

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-four-layer-memory.mmd" title="Four-layer memory architecture" /%}
*Figure 4. Four-layer memory model: intellectual, execution, institutional and temporary provider context.*

| Memory layer | What it contains | Authority / retention |
|---|---|---|
| Human intellectual memory | Thoughts, questions, hypotheses, evidence, contradictions, mental models, understanding evolution. | Durable/versioned; Phase 1.6 owns. |
| Execution memory | WorkUnits, gates, candidate SHA, PR, provider status, next action. | Durable/bounded; inherited from Phase 1.5. |
| Institutional memory | Accepted ADRs, architecture, policies, contracts, runbooks, outcomes, retrospectives. | Canonical and durable. |
| Provider context | Claude/Codex working windows, tool context, scratch computation. | Ephemeral optimization; never authoritative. |

### 6.1 Memory compilation rule

Compilers select the smallest relevant package for machine execution or human
understanding. References are preferred over duplicated content.

---

## 7. Research Thread and question decomposition

A Research Thread is the durable container for one meaningful question. It preserves
the original thought, the formalized question, the changing evidence set, the
understanding history, decision status, and later execution/outcome links.

| Question type | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Core question | The precise problem the user is trying to understand or decide. |
| Current-state question | What is actually true in Beacon now? |
| Mechanism question | Why does the system or phenomenon behave this way? |
| Alternative question | What credible alternatives exist? |
| Evidence question | What would make the claim more or less believable? |
| Boundary question | Where does the model stop being reliable? |
| Decision question | What would have to be true before an ADR is justified? |

> **SEARCH BEFORE SYNTHESIS.** A Research Thread should not begin by asking a model
> to write an answer.

---

## 8. Evidence acquisition and source hierarchy

1. Current Beacon implementation and real project evidence for questions about
   Beacon itself.
2. Primary technical sources: official specifications, vendor documentation,
   standards, source code.
3. Original peer-reviewed research for empirical or theoretical claims.
4. Systematic reviews and meta-analyses.
5. Credible open-source implementations, issues, design histories.
6. Community/practitioner evidence for failure modes, adoption friction, lived
   experience.

```
Evidence {
  id, sourceRef, retrievedAt, sourceType,
  claimRefs[], supports[], contradicts[],
  qualitySignals, applicabilityToBeacon,
  freshnessClass, staleAfter?,
  excerptRef?, artifactHash?, notes
}
```

---

## 8A. Source Identity and Content Hashing *(new in V6.5)*

`Evidence.artifactHash` already existed; what was missing is identity for the
**source** itself, not just the evidence excerpt taken from it. The distinction
matters because **a URL is not a content identity** — a webpage can change after
Beacon retrieves it, without its address changing at all. Without a content hash
at the source level, "what exactly did Beacon see when it made this claim" is
unanswerable after the fact, which directly undermines INV-004's provenance
requirement.

```
SourceRecord {
  sourceId, canonicalLocator,
  publisher, title, publicationDate?,
  retrievedAt, contentHash,
  versionIdentifier?, sourceType,
  supersedesSourceRef?
}
```

`contentHash` is computed over the retrieved content at `retrievedAt`, not the
locator. Two `Evidence` records citing the same `canonicalLocator` but different
`contentHash` values are citing **different sources** that happen to share an
address — they are never silently merged. `supersedesSourceRef` lets a source
record be superseded the same way an ADR is (Section 19A) when a re-fetch shows
the content changed, preserving the old hash as history rather than overwriting
it.

---

## 9. Evidence freshness and staleness

> **This section's principle is the one that Addendum V1 (folded into Section 29A)
> extends to provider-continuity state — the same rule, applied to the same category
> of fact, closing a gap this project hit twice in one day.**

| Freshness class | Examples | Default treatment |
|---|---|---|
| Foundational | Classic cognitive theory, stable mathematics. | Age alone does not make it stale. |
| Slow-changing | Architecture practices, mature APIs. | Revalidate at important decisions or after ecosystem change. |
| Current | Product documentation, tool behavior, repo state. | Bind to retrieval date/version; re-check before decision. |
| Fast-changing | Pricing, product limits, policies, software versions. | Short revalidation window; refresh before consequential decisions. |
| **Ephemeral** | **Incidents, live status, deployment state, provider capacity, gate results.** | **Treat as timestamped observation only. Never trust on age alone.** |

```
UnderstandingVersion {
  ...
  projectContextHash,
  evidenceSetHash,
  claimLedgerVersion,
  sourceSnapshotAt
}

If material evidence changes -> create a new UnderstandingVersion.
Never silently rewrite the old one.
```

---

## 10. Claim Ledger and contradiction engine

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-claim-ledger-engine.mmd" title="Claim Ledger and contradiction engine" /%}
*Figure 5. Sources are decomposed into claims, tested across evidence lanes, then synthesized into versioned understanding.*

| Claim status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| SUPPORTED | Strong evidence supports the claim in the relevant scope. |
| SUPPORTED_WITH_LIMITS | Core claim useful but has material boundary conditions. |
| CONTESTED | Credible evidence supports materially different interpretations. |
| UNRESOLVED | Evidence insufficient, conflicting or not yet applicable. |
| SUPERSEDED | A later formulation better represents the evidence. |
| REJECTED | Unsupported, contradicted, or materially misleading in scope. |

### 10.1 Anti-confirmation rule

- For decision-relevant claims, actively search for credible contradictory evidence.
- Preserve supporting and contradicting evidence together.
- Do not flatten disagreement into a false single certainty.
- Model confidence is not evidence quality.
- Unknown or contested claims remain visible in the Understanding Package and
  Decision Candidate.

---

## 11. UnderstandingVersion model

```
UnderstandingVersion {
  id, threadId, createdAt,
  summary30s,
  coreMechanism,
  deepModelRef,
  mentalModelRefs[],
  boundaryConditions[],
  contradictionRefs[],
  unresolvedQuestions[],
  observableUnderstandingChecks[],
  derivedFromEvidenceRefs[],
  evidenceSetHash,
  projectContextHash,
  supersedesVersionRef?
}
```

> **HISTORY IS AN ASSET.** Beacon should preserve not only what was decided, but why
> the mental model changed.

---

## 12. Evidence-Based Understanding Engine

| Understanding layer | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Question | What exactly are we trying to understand? |
| 30-second model | Smallest accurate orientation. |
| Concrete anchor / analogy | Optional bridge to prior knowledge; state where it breaks. |
| Minimal working model | Smallest system that demonstrates the mechanism. |
| Mechanism | Why the behavior occurs. |
| Best visual | Representation selected by knowledge shape + learning purpose. |
| Interactive exploration | Only when manipulation creates useful cognitive work. |
| Active check | Prediction, explanation, retrieval, comparison, construction, transfer. |
| Boundary conditions | Where the simplified model fails. |
| Deep technical model | Implementation/theory details on demand. |
| Evidence + contradictions | Why to believe the model and where disagreement remains. |
| Connections | Related concepts, ADRs, PRs, experiments, outcomes. |

---

## 13. Understanding Compiler

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-understanding-compiler.mmd" title="Understanding Compiler" /%}
*Figure 6. The Understanding Compiler creates the smallest useful human mental model from versioned knowledge.*

```
UnderstandingPackage {
  conceptRef,
  understandingVersionRef,
  summary30s,
  prerequisiteRefs[],
  mechanism,
  boundaryConditions[],
  recommendedVisuals[],
  interactionSpec?,
  activeChecks[],
  contradictionRefs[],
  evidenceRefs[],
  deepReferenceRefs[]
}
```

---

## 14. Human cognition and learning-science policy

| Principle | Beacon policy | Rejected oversimplification |
|---|---|---|
| Working memory / cognitive load | Manage complexity relative to prior knowledge; progressively disclose. | "Never show more than four things." |
| Dual coding / multimedia | Combine verbal and visual when each contributes meaning. | "Always add an image." |
| Spatial contiguity / signaling | Keep labels near relevant visual when it reduces searching. | "More annotation is always better." |
| Segmenting | Let complex sequences unfold in learner-paced steps when needed. | "Everything must be a stepper." |
| Active/generative processing | Prefer prediction, explanation, construction, comparison. | "More clicking means more learning." |
| Retrieval practice | Use retrieval as strong evidence of learning. | "Retrieval always beats concept maps." |
| Feedback | Perceptually immediate for direct manipulation; task-dependent timing otherwise. | "All feedback must arrive within milliseconds." |
| Analogy | Use when structural mapping reduces conceptual distance; disclose breakpoints. | "Abstract concepts cannot be understood without analogy." |
| Concrete-to-abstract | Use concreteness fading when it fits; not a universal order. | "Every concept must follow the same sequence." |

### 14.1 Human memory vs Beacon memory

Beacon does not claim to store a human brain. It stores externalized intellectual
lineage so the human does not have to reconstruct every decision from memory.

---

## 15. Visual Understanding Router

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-visual-understanding-router.mmd" title="Visual Understanding Router" /%}
*Figure 7. Representation is chosen by both knowledge structure and learning intent.*

| Knowledge / learning need | Preferred representation |
|---|---|
| Hierarchy / concept landscape | Markmap or equivalent mind-map projection. |
| Process / architecture / sequence / state / ERD | Mermaid canonical diagram. |
| Exploratory design reasoning | Excalidraw, clearly marked exploratory/non-authoritative. |
| Alternatives / evidence / readiness | Beacon-native matrix or checklist. |
| Evolution of understanding | Timeline with version diffs. |
| Quantitative behavior | Chart with explicit source/provenance. |
| Causal variable relationship | Bounded interactive simulation if manipulation reveals mechanism. |
| Text is clearest | Use text. No mandatory visual. |

> **EXPLORABLE-FIRST, NOT TEXT-FIRST *(new in V6.2)*.** For any concept extracted
> from an accepted or candidate decision, the router's default is an interactive
> **explorable explanation** — manipulable, progressive-disclosure content per
> Section 17A — not static prose. Static text is the documented fallback for
> concepts where manipulation adds no comprehension value, not the baseline every
> other representation has to justify displacing.

---

## 16. Diagram architecture and representation canon

| Diagram type | Canonical use | Authority rule |
|---|---|---|
| Markmap | Concept landscapes, prerequisite trees, research question structures. | Learning/navigation projection; not implementation authority. |
| Mermaid flow/sequence/state/ERD/architecture | Canonical technical relationships and execution flows. | May become canonical when linked to accepted ADR/docs. |
| Excalidraw | Exploration, analogy, whiteboarding, uncertain architecture. | Always exploratory until promoted through an accepted decision. |
| Native Beacon matrix/timeline | Evidence, alternatives, readiness, evolution, status. | Derived projection from canonical objects. |
| Interactive model | Causal exploration where state manipulation materially improves understanding. | Explanatory, never execution authority by itself. |

### 16.1 Promotion from exploratory to formal architecture

```
Excalidraw exploration
  -> evidence / alternatives
  -> Decision Candidate
  -> human acceptance
  -> accepted ADR
  -> canonical Mermaid architecture / docs
```

---

## 17. Active understanding, retrieval and feedback

- Predict what happens next.
- Change one variable and explain the consequence.
- Explain the mechanism in plain language.
- Retrieve a core relationship without rereading.
- Compare two models and identify where each fails.
- Apply the concept to a new Beacon scenario.
- Diagnose an intentionally incorrect architecture or claim.

| Evidence | State |
|---|---|
| Can identify core components | yes / no / unknown |
| Can explain simply | yes / no / unknown |
| Can predict behavior | yes / no / unknown |
| Can distinguish credible alternatives | yes / no / unknown |
| Can identify boundary cases | yes / no / unknown |
| Can apply to a new case | yes / no / unknown |
| Can explain why a rejected option fails | yes / no / unknown |

> Do not collapse these into an invented 94% understanding score. Unknown remains
> unknown.

---

## 17A. Prompt-to-Learning-Journey Pipeline *(new in V6.2, Addendum V4)*

### 17A.1 What this formalizes

Every terminal prompt already enters the lifecycle in Section 4 as a
`PromptObserved` event. What was not previously explicit: when that prompt's
underlying concept resolves toward a decision, the concept should become an
**explorable explanation** — the established term (Bret Victor, 2011; Appendix B
[R14]) for interactive content that lets a learner manipulate the concept and
observe consequences, rather than only reading a description of it. This is not a
new subsystem — it is the existing Visual Understanding Router (Section 15) and
Understanding Compiler (Section 13) with an explicit default and a source-decision
link that were previously left implicit.

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-prompt-to-learning-journey.mmd" title="Prompt-to-Learning-Journey pipeline" /%}
*Figure 8A. Pipeline from a captured terminal prompt to a versioned, interactive learning journey.*

1. **Prompt + decision context.** A `PromptObserved` event that resolves toward a
   `Claim` or `DecisionCandidate` (Section 4 steps 6-9) carries a reference to that
   decision forward, not just the raw prompt text.
2. **Concept extraction.** Identify what specifically needs explaining — the
   mechanism, the trade-off, the failure mode being avoided — not a restatement of
   the decision's conclusion. This reuses the Understanding Compiler's existing
   summarization step (Section 13), scoped to teaching rather than to
   `DecisionReadiness`.
3. **Visual Understanding Router,** now explorable-first per Section 15's updated
   default: an interactive, manipulable representation unless text is
   demonstrably clearer for this specific concept.
4. **Progressive disclosure + retrieval check.** Overview first, mathematical or
   architectural detail revealed on interaction — the pattern CHI 2026's
   Transformer Explainer uses for exactly this kind of layered technical
   concept (Appendix B [R15]) — combined with the active-understanding checks
   already defined in Section 17.
5. **Versioned learning package.** Stored as a `LearningPackage` (Appendix A),
   linked to the specific `UnderstandingVersion` and `ADRRef` version that
   produced it — see Section 19A for what happens to this package when the
   underlying ADR is superseded.

### 17A.2 What this is not

This pipeline does not run on every prompt unconditionally — most terminal
interaction is operational (Section 4 step 2: continuation, status, routine
work) and never reaches concept extraction. It activates only where a prompt's
classification already places it on the research/understanding path.

---

## 17B. Explorable-Explanation Evaluation Checkpoint *(new in V6.2, Addendum V4)*

Auto-generated interactive content can render successfully and still fail to
teach anything — a functioning widget is not evidence of a pedagogically sound
one. EE-Eval, a 2026 framework evaluated across 127 concepts and six AI
models, found that interaction quality requires assessment as testable
behavioral structure — what states a learner can reach, what actions are
available, how actions change outcomes — rather than being inferred from surface
correctness or visual polish (Appendix B [R16]).

Before a `LearningPackage` is marked trusted (as opposed to draft), check:

| Check | Question |
|---|---|
| Reachable states | Can a learner actually get to every state the explanation claims to show, or are some states dead ends? |
| Action-consequence clarity | Does manipulating a variable produce a visible, attributable change, or is the connection ambiguous? |
| Progressive disclosure integrity | Does the overview stand alone as correct, or does it silently depend on detail the learner hasn't seen yet? |
| Retrieval alignment | Do the active-understanding checks (Section 17) actually probe what the explorable explanation demonstrated, or a different concept? |

> **HONEST SCOPING.** Per operating law 3: this checklist is a planning-stage
> adaptation of EE-Eval's published framework, not a validated Beacon
> implementation of it. No `LearningPackage` has been run through this checkpoint
> against a real learner yet — see Section 0.5.

---

## 18. Decision readiness and Decision Candidate

```
DecisionReadiness {
  problemDefined,
  currentStateVerified,
  primaryEvidencePresent,
  credibleAlternativesCompared,
  contradictionsHandled,
  securityConsidered,
  costComplexityConsidered,
  architectureImpactKnown,
  experimentDispositionKnown,
  revisitConditionsDefined,
  readyForAdr
}
```

| Disposition | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Accept | Create `DecisionAccepted` and begin ADR request; still not executable until ADR accepted. |
| Continue Research | Return to the Research Thread with explicit unresolved questions. |
| Reject | Record why the candidate was rejected; preserve evidence and reconsideration triggers. |

---

## 19. ADR authority boundary

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-adr-authority-boundary.mmd" title="ADR authority boundary" /%}
*Figure 8. Only a human-accepted ADR crosses the research-to-execution authority boundary.*

> **AUTHORITY INVARIANT.** Research != authorization. Evidence != authorization.
> Concept != authorization. Diagram != authorization. Recommendation != authorization.
> Decision Candidate != authorization. Only an accepted ADR authorizes execution.

---

## 19A. ADR Supersession and Version Lineage *(new in V6.2, Addendum V4)*

### 19A.1 The contract

Matches established industry practice rather than inventing a Beacon-specific
scheme (Appendix B [R17]-[R19]):

```
ADRRef {
  ...existing fields...
  status: "proposed" | "accepted" | "deprecated" | "superseded",
  supersedesAdrRef?,      ← points backward to the decision this replaces
  supersededByAdrRef?,    ← points forward once a replacement is accepted
  immutableAfterAcceptance: true
}
```

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-adr-supersession-lifecycle.mmd" title="ADR supersession and version lineage" /%}
*Figure 8B. ADR lifecycle. An accepted ADR is never edited in place — a changed decision produces a new ADR that supersedes it, with links in both directions.*

### 19A.2 Binding rules

1. **Immutability after acceptance.** An accepted ADR's original content is never
   edited. If new evidence or a changed decision emerges, write a new ADR.
2. **Supersession is bidirectional and explicit.** The new ADR sets
   `supersedesAdrRef`; the old ADR's `status` becomes `superseded` and
   `supersededByAdrRef` is set — both directions are machine-checkable, not
   dependent on someone remembering to update a status field by convention.
3. **History is preserved, never deleted.** A superseded ADR remains reachable at
   its original location. This is the direct mechanism satisfying the "history
   preserver" requirement this addendum was built around.

### 19A.3 Effect on the learning layer

A `LearningPackage` (Appendix A) is linked to the specific `ADRRef` version that
produced it, not just to the concept. When an ADR is superseded:

- The existing `LearningPackage` is marked historical and stays reachable — the
  old explanation of the old decision does not silently vanish or get
  overwritten.
- A new `LearningPackage` is generated against the new ADR through the Section
  17A pipeline and linked forward.
- The learning site (Section 24A) surfaces the supersession relationship
  directly — a learner reading the old lesson sees that it was superseded and
  can navigate to the current one, rather than the old page simply looking
  authoritative and current.

---

## 20. Compact Decision Package and Phase 1.5 handoff

```
DecisionPackage {
  adrId,
  objective,
  acceptanceCriteria[],
  invariants[],
  constraints[],
  securityRequirements[],
  risk,
  architectureRefs[],
  documentationRefs[],
  evidenceRefs[],
  unresolvedRisks[],
  revisitConditions[]
}
```

Phase 1.5 then performs its existing Chief-of-Staff → WorkUnit → context compiler →
provider router → eight agents → QA/review/council → CI/publication/release
pipeline. Phase 1.6 must not bypass this path.

---

## 21. End-to-end provenance and auditability

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-provenance-flow.mmd" title="End-to-end provenance flow" /%}
*Figure 9. Forward provenance from source to production; every step is traceable backward as well.*

### 21.1 Provenance invariants

- Every Claim references Evidence; every Evidence references a Source or project
  artifact.
- Every UnderstandingVersion records its evidence set and project-context hash.
- Every Decision Candidate references its source UnderstandingVersion.
- Every accepted ADR links back to the Decision Candidate and forward to
  DecisionPackage/WorkUnits.
- Every deployment/result references the immutable artifact and originating decision
  lineage.
- Retrospectives can create new claims/evidence and therefore new
  UnderstandingVersions.

---

## 22. Terminal UX

The terminal is the primary control plane.

```
$ beacon
Project     Beacon-Co
Phase       1.6
Knowledge   synchronized
Providers   Phase 1.5 broker
Provider    Claude (approaching limit — 12% remaining)     ← new in V5
Continuity  fresh (observed 3m ago)                         ← new in V5

> Would a knowledge graph materially improve our retrieval?

Beacon:
- captured thought
- attached current project context
- found existing related thread: knowledge-relations
- 3 unresolved questions
- research can continue without opening /lab
```

**On an automatic provider switch (new in V5):**
```
Beacon: Claude capacity reached mid-task.
  → Continuation package generated (observedAt: 2026-08-10T14:32:01Z)
  → Switching to Codex
  → Codex: context loaded, resuming from last WorkUnit step
  → No user action required.
```

**When both providers are unavailable (new in V5):**
```
Beacon: Both Claude and Codex are at capacity.
  → Work paused, ContinuationPackage saved and current.
  → No provider action possible right now.
  → Resume with `beacon continue` once either provider is available.
```

### 22.1 Core commands

| Command | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `beacon status` | Phase, provider, readiness, current WorkUnit/Research Thread. |
| `beacon knowledge <topic>` | Retrieve current understanding and linked evidence. |
| `beacon research <question>` | Create/continue a Research Thread. |
| `beacon explain <concept>` | Compile an Understanding Package. |
| `beacon decision` | Inspect decision readiness and current candidate. |
| `beacon continue` | Resume from provider-neutral continuation state — re-verifies freshness before trusting gate status (V5). |
| `beacon lineage <id>` | Trace thought/evidence/ADR/implementation/outcome provenance. |

---

## 23. Beacon Lab UX

`/lab` is a rich projection for learning and review — not the canonical database, not
a manual wiki maintenance surface.

### 23.1 Concept / thread page
- Original question and current status
- 30-second model
- Question Tree and related concepts
- Current UnderstandingVersion with version history
- Claim Ledger: supported / limited / contested / rejected
- Evidence matrix with source type, quality and freshness
- Best-fit diagrams and interactive explanation when justified
- Boundary conditions and unresolved questions
- Observable understanding checks
- Alternatives and Decision Readiness
- Linked ADRs, PRs, WorkUnits, deployments and outcomes

### 23.2 Progressive disclosure

The first viewport should orient, not overwhelm.

---

## 24. Private, formal and public projections

| Projection | Visibility | Content policy |
|---|---|---|
| Beacon Lab / Brain | Private by default | Raw questions, incomplete research, contradictions, experiments, internal reasoning. |
| Decisions / Docs | Internal/canonical | Accepted ADRs and current implementation truth. |
| Building Beacon | Public curated | Sanitized narrative only; no raw prompts, secrets, customer data, security-sensitive details, unverified claims. |

> **PUBLICATION IS A PROJECTION.** Never make public publication the storage
> mechanism for private intellectual history.

---

## 24A. Three-Site Deployment Model *(new in V6.2, Addendum V4)*

### 24A.1 Three sites, one canonical lineage

The projections in Section 24 are formalized here as three actually-deployed
properties rather than sections of a single site. Each reads from the same
`ResearchThread → Claim → UnderstandingVersion → ADRRef` lineage (Section 25) —
none of the three authors its own separate copy.

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-three-site-deployment.mmd" title="Three-site deployment model" /%}
*Figure 8C. Three deployed sites projecting from one canonical lineage — none is a separately-maintained copy.*

| Site | Maps to Section 3/24 surface | Content | Authority |
|---|---|---|---|
| Business site | `/building` | Sanitized public narrative of what was learned, decided, built, observed. | Narrative only — never authoritative for implementation or decisions. |
| Decisions site | `/decisions` + `/docs` | Accepted ADRs (with Section 19A's supersession chain visible), current architecture, contracts, runbooks. | Formal — accepted ADRs are the execution authority boundary (Section 19). |
| Learning site | Beacon Lab's learning half | Explorable explanations produced by the Section 17A pipeline, versioned per Section 19A.3. | Explanatory — teaches why and how, never itself authorizes execution. |

### 24A.2 Why this doesn't create three maintenance burdens

Because all three read the same lineage, a decision only needs to be made once
(through the existing Section 18-19 flow) to appear correctly on all three sites:
the decisions site gets the formal ADR, the learning site gets the explorable
explanation generated from it (Section 17A), and the business site gets a
sanitized narrative excerpt — each is a different **projection rule** over the
same underlying event-sourced history (Section 5), not a different authoring
process.

---

## 24B. Deployment Mechanics for the Three Sites *(new in V6.3, resolves the open item left in V6.2)*

### 24B.1 One monorepo, one canonical content layer, three build targets

The choice is between three separately-maintained repositories (each risking its
own drift and its own copy of the lineage) and one monorepo with three build
targets reading a shared, generated content layer. This document takes the
second option, because it is the same principle Section 3 already states —
**one lineage, multiple projections** — applied to infrastructure rather than
just to data modeling. A separate-repos approach would silently reintroduce the
"four manually synchronized knowledge bases" failure mode Section 3 already
rejects, just at the deployment layer instead of the content layer.

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-deployment-mechanics.mmd" title="Deployment mechanics for the three sites" /%}
*Figure 8D. One monorepo and one canonical content layer, filtered at build time into three separate deploy targets — not three separately authored repositories.*

### 24B.2 What the projection filter actually does

The filter step is where Section 27's existing visibility/publication controls
become concrete per site, reusing the same enforcement point rather than adding
three new ones:

| Build target | Filter rule | Reuses |
|---|---|---|
| Business | Strip anything not explicitly marked publication-ready; sanitize per Section 27. | Existing `scripts/ci/prepublish.mjs` gate (Appendix C), already authoritative for `/building`. |
| Decisions | Include only `accepted`, `deprecated`, and `superseded` ADRs (never `proposed`) plus current `/docs` truth. | Section 19's authority boundary — a proposed ADR cannot appear here any more than it can authorize execution. |
| Learning | Include only `LearningPackage` records with `status: trusted` (Section 17B) or `status: historical` (superseded, per 19A.3) — never `draft`. | The same status-gating pattern Section 19A already applies to ADRs, applied to `LearningPackage` instead. |

### 24B.3 Why this is a build-time filter, not three separate CMSs

Each site's filter rule is a deterministic function over the same canonical
records — same discipline as Phase 1.5's "deterministic gates before model
judgment" (inherited constitution, Section 2). A record's visibility status
changes once, at the source (an ADR is accepted, a LearningPackage passes 17B's
checklist), and all three sites' next build picks up that change automatically.
Nobody edits the business site, the decisions site, and the learning site
separately when one ADR is accepted.

### 24B.4 Honest scoping

Per operating law 3: this section specifies **which** filter rules apply to each
site and **why** a shared build is preferred over three repos. It does not
specify hosting provider, subdomain structure, or CI trigger configuration —
those are ordinary infrastructure choices with no research dependency, and
belong in an implementation PR, not this document.

---

## 25. Canonical data model and ERD

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-canonical-data-model.mmd" title="Canonical data model (ERD)" /%}
*Figure 10. Simplified Phase 1.6 knowledge-to-authority data relationships, extended with ContinuationPackage tracking (V5) and ADR supersession + LearningPackage generation (V6.2).*

Initial persistence should use explicit typed relations and generated indexes that
extend the existing Markdoc/project-memory system. A graph database is not required
until measured query/retrieval requirements justify it.

### 25.1 Minimum canonical entities

- PromptObserved / ThoughtClassification
- ResearchThread / Question
- ProjectContextSnapshot
- Source / Evidence
- Claim / ClaimRelation
- UnderstandingVersion / MentalModel / UnderstandingCheck
- Alternative / Experiment
- DecisionReadiness / DecisionCandidate
- ADRRef / DecisionPackage
- ExecutionRef / OutcomeRef / Retrospective
- **ContinuationPackage** *(promoted to canonical entity in V5 — was implicit in V4)*
- **LearningPackage** *(new canonical entity in V6.2 — versioned per ADR, see Section 19A.3)*

