## 26. Event vocabulary and schemas

| Lifecycle area | Events |
|---|---|
| Thought | PromptObserved, ThoughtClassified, ResearchThreadCreated, ResearchThreadUpdated |
| Research | QuestionFormalized, QuestionAdded, ProjectContextAttached, ResearchPlanCreated, ResearchStarted, SourceFound, SourceEvaluated, EvidenceAdded, EvidenceContradicted |
| Understanding | ClaimCreated, ClaimStatusChanged, AlternativeAdded, ExperimentProposed, ExperimentCompleted, MentalModelSelected, VisualGenerated, UnderstandingVersionCreated |
| Decision | DecisionReadinessUpdated, DecisionCandidateCreated, DecisionAccepted, DecisionRejected, ADRRequested, ADRAccepted, ArchitectureImpactCalculated, DocumentationImpactCalculated, DecisionPackageCreated |
| Execution feedback | WorkUnitCreated, AgentRunStarted, AgentRunCompleted, QAPassed, ReviewCompleted, CouncilCompleted, CIPassed, ReleaseVerified, OutcomeObserved, RetrospectiveCreated, **ContinuationPackageGenerated, ContinuationPackageStale** *(new in V5)* |
| **Provider continuity** *(new lifecycle area in V5)* | **ProviderCapacityWarning, ProviderSwitchTriggered, ProviderSwitchCompleted, BothProvidersUnavailable** |
| Narrative | StoryCandidateCreated, StoryPublished |

```
KnowledgeEvent<T> {
  eventId,
  eventType,
  occurredAt,
  actorRef,
  projectRef,
  visibility,
  aggregateRef,
  projectContextRef?,
  causationRef?,
  correlationRef?,
  payload: T
}
```

---

## 26A. Event and Replay Semantics *(new in V6.5)*

`causationRef` and `correlationRef` already exist on every event (above); this
section states the rules that make them load-bearing rather than optional
metadata.

1. **Identity.** `eventId` MUST be globally unique. It is the sole basis for
   deduplication — nothing downstream may re-derive identity from payload
   content.
2. **Idempotency.** If an event with a given `eventId` is received more than
   once, only the first occurrence may produce a state transition. A duplicate
   delivery is a no-op, not an error and not a second transition.
3. **Causation.** `causationRef` answers "what event caused this event" —
   required whenever an event is a direct consequence of processing another
   event (e.g. `ADRAccepted` causing `DecisionPackageCreated`).
4. **Correlation.** `correlationRef` answers "what larger operation does this
   event belong to" — the thread that groups a whole WorkUnit's events together
   even when several are not directly causally linked to each other.
5. **Replay.** Beacon MUST be able to rebuild all derived/projected state
   (Section 24A's three sites, any read model) purely from the canonical event
   log. This is the acceptance test for the event model, not an aspiration —
   Section 33's eval strategy should include a fixture that deletes a derived
   projection and confirms replay reconstructs it byte-for-byte equivalent.

---

## 27. Security, privacy and publication controls

- Private by default for raw prompts, research threads, incomplete understanding.
- Do not persist provider credentials, raw secrets, unnecessary provider metadata,
  or complete transcripts merely for continuity.
- Source excerpts bounded and copyright-aware.
- Customer or personal data requires explicit classification and access controls.
- Research tools have no deployment authority.
- Decision acceptance and publication are separate human-controlled actions.
- All Phase 1.6 execution changes inherit Phase 1.5 tool policy, publication
  readiness, protected checks, attestation and environment approval controls.

---

## 27A. Data Classification Matrix *(new in V6.5)*

Section 29B.4 already tiers providers by trust (T0-T4). What was missing is the
explicit connection between that tier and what data it may see — this table
makes INV-007 checkable rather than aspirational.

| Data classification | Paid trusted (T1) | Vetted free (T1-equivalent) | Experimental (T3) | Public |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Public | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| Internal | ✓ | policy-gated | ✗ | ✗ |
| Confidential | approved only | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ |
| Secrets | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ |
| Production credentials | role-controlled | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ |

> **BINDING RULE.** Routing precedence step 2 (Section 29B.5, "security/
> data-classification eligibility") is this table, not a separate judgment call.
> A provider fails eligibility at step 2 — before task-class lookup, before
> health, before cost — if the WorkUnit's data classification is not permitted
> for that provider's trust tier. No downstream ranking criterion can override a
> step-2 failure.

---

## 28. Token, context and provider-cap discipline

| Tactic | Rule |
|---|---|
| Search before read | Locate relevant claims/docs/code before opening full sources. |
| Reference first | Pass stable IDs/paths/URLs; copy exact text only when needed. |
| Context deduplication | Do not resend unchanged project background across every step. |
| Canonical summaries | Reuse current UnderstandingVersion/DecisionPackage instead of re-summarizing. |
| Provider-native caching | Use and measure where supported; don't make provider cache the source of truth. |
| Conditional token auditor | Invoke when budget/routing decisions are material, not on every trivial action. |
| No premature embeddings/RAG | Measure retrieval failure before adding semantic infrastructure. |
| **Predictive capacity monitoring** *(new in V5)* | **Watch usage trend toward a limit, not just react to the error after it's hit — see Section 29A.** |

---

## 29. Provider-neutral continuity

The current Phase 1.5 continuation model is reused. Phase 1.6 adds
research/understanding references to continuation state.

```
ContinuationPackage {
  businessGoal,
  activeResearchThreadRef?,
  understandingVersionRef?,
  acceptedAdrRef?,
  decisionPackageRef?,
  workUnitRef?,
  changedPaths[],
  completedGates[], failedGates[], outstandingGates[],
  localReady, publicationReady, externalReady,
  candidateSha?, prNumber?,
  nextAuthorizedAction,

  observedAt,                    ← new in V5
  observedMainSha,                ← new in V5
  freshnessClass: "ephemeral",    ← new in V5, fixed value
  staleAfter                      ← new in V5
}
```

> **PROVIDER CONTEXT IS DISPOSABLE.** Claude and Codex sessions are temporary
> optimizations. A provider switch must not erase the user's reasoning history or
> require re-reading the whole repository if Beacon already owns the relevant state.

---

## 29A. Continuity Freshness Discipline and the Capacity Watcher *(new section in V5, folds in Addendum V1 in full)*

### 29A.1 Why this section exists — real evidence, not speculation

Two real incidents in one project day justify this, per operating law 6:

1. **Observation-file staleness.**
   `agent-platform/baselines/external-controls-observation-2026-08-09.json` was
   hand-written once and read by `phase15:audit` repeatedly with zero re-verification
   against live GitHub state. It happened to still be accurate when finally checked
   — nothing in the system would have caught it if it hadn't been.
2. **Handoff-file staleness.** `.ai/handoff.md` was written before PR #28 merged,
   said "not merged yet," and sat stale for roughly a day and a half, including
   through an entire separate investigation thread, before anyone noticed.

Both share the same shape: a snapshot of `Ephemeral`-class data (Section 9) was
treated as durable. The freshness principle already existed in this document —
nothing enforced it against the document's own continuity objects.

### 29A.2 Binding revalidation rule

> **Any consumer of a `ContinuationPackage` — human or agent — MUST treat its
> readiness/gate fields as untrusted once `observedAt + staleAfter` has passed, and
> MUST re-run the live source-of-truth check (`npm run phase15:audit`, live `gh api`
> calls, etc.) before acting on them. A stale `ContinuationPackage` may still be used
> for narrative/business-goal continuity — never for gate-status decisions.**

### 29A.3 The Capacity Watcher

Phase 1.6 owns a new subsystem: predictive, automatic, bidirectional provider
handoff, addressing four gaps the original V4.0 design left open:

1. **Prediction, not just reaction** — a warning threshold, distinct from the hard
   cap, generates a fresh `ContinuationPackage` *before* the limit is hit.
2. **Automatic trigger** — hitting the hard cap switches providers without a human
   needing to notice and run a command.
3. **Bidirectional, repeatable loop** — not a one-time handoff; can happen multiple
   times in one work session, in either direction.
4. **Defined terminal state** — when both providers are capped, Beacon pauses,
   persists a freshly-generated `ContinuationPackage`, reports plainly, and waits.
   It does not poll paid provider quota unattended; resuming requires an explicit
   `beacon continue`.

{% mermaid_diagram src="/diagrams/mermaid/phase-1-6-capacity-watcher-sequence.mmd" title="Capacity Watcher sequence" /%}
*Figure 11. Capacity Watcher: predictive monitoring, automatic bidirectional switching, defined pause state.*

### 29A.4 Honest scoping — what is not yet proven

Per operating law 3 (as clarified in Section 1.1) and this project's own precedent
of refusing to fabricate a session-limit incident that never happened: **as of V5.0,
no real Claude-session-cap or Codex-rate-limit event has actually been observed in
this project.** This design is precautionary. Its implementation PR's exit evidence
must be **simulated** scenarios, clearly labeled as such, until a real capacity event
is observed to trigger correctly.

### 29A.5 New events (see Section 26)

`ContinuationPackageGenerated`, `ContinuationPackageStale`,
`ProviderCapacityWarning`, `ProviderSwitchTriggered`, `ProviderSwitchCompleted`,
`BothProvidersUnavailable`.

### 29A.6 Open decisions (not yet resolved — do not treat as settled)

1. ~~What usage signal does each provider actually expose for the Watcher to
   poll?~~ **PARTIALLY RESOLVED in V6.5.1 — see Section 29A.7.** Claude Code
   confirmed; Codex not yet independently verified.
2. `staleAfter`'s default duration (currently placeholder: one session, never > 4h)
   should be set from real usage data once available.
3. PR numbering: insert as PR-0.5, or renumber the full PR-0–PR-8 sequence (Section
   31).

### 29A.7 Prioritization, reconsidered *(new in V6.5.1)*

Two things changed since 29A.6 item 1 was written, and they point in different
directions — worth stating both rather than only the convenient one.

**What's newly confirmed:** Claude Code's `/usage` command and its underlying
`GET /api/oauth/usage` endpoint return structured, machine-readable fields —
`five_hour.utilization` and `seven_day.utilization` as percentages, plus
`resets_at` timestamps (dated 2026, cross-confirmed across independent technical
write-ups, Appendix C). This resolves the technical half of open decision 1 for
Claude Code specifically: a queryable signal exists. Codex's equivalent has not
been independently confirmed by this document and should not be assumed
equivalent without checking.

**What that does not resolve:** technical feasibility is not the same question
as implementation priority. Section 29A.4 already states plainly that no real
capacity-cap event has triggered this mechanism in Beacon's own use. A feasible
signal to poll doesn't create a demonstrated, repeated problem worth building
against yet.

```
Capacity Watcher ──┬── actual repeated cap problem observed?
                    └── measurable workflow interruption?
                              │
                          NO  │
                              ↓
                         EXPERIMENT, not blocker
```

**Resolution:** the Capacity Watcher's design stays as specified — nothing about
it was wrong. Its *position in the PR sequence* moves: Section 31's PR-0.5 is now
an evidence-gated side track off PR-0B, not a step on the main critical path (see
Section 31's updated diagram). The **freshness half** of provider continuity
(revalidating a `ContinuationPackage` before trusting it) stays on the critical
path immediately, in PR-1, because that rule doesn't depend on the predictive
capacity-monitoring mechanism at all — only the *automatic predictive switch*
half is deferred pending evidence that the problem recurs often enough to justify
building it now rather than later.

---

## 29B. Multi-Provider Task Routing and Free-Tier Capability Extension *(new section in V6, Addendum V2)*

### 29B.1 What gap this closes

Section 29A answers *"what happens when the provider in use runs out of capacity."*
It does not answer *"which provider should run this task in the first place."*
Today, Claude and Codex are treated as interchangeable under one shared role policy
(Appendix, Phase 1.5 agent contracts) — the only thing that moves work from one to
the other is a capacity cap. There is no task-fit dimension.

This section adds that second, independent dimension: a rule-based classifier that
runs *before* provider selection on every WorkUnit, plus a task-class → provider
table that includes free-tier candidates as first-class routing options — not
merely as an overflow valve for when Claude and Codex are both capped. Free-tier
candidates are modeled as two independently swappable parts, a harness (the CLI
tool, e.g. OpenCode or Aider) and a backend (the model API it calls, e.g. Groq,
Cerebras, or Google AI Studio) — see Section 29B.10 for why this split matters and
what replaced the originally-planned Gemini CLI candidate.

> **TASK FIT IS NOT CAPACITY.** A provider can be fully available and still be the
> wrong choice for a given task class. A provider can be free-tier and still be the
> *right* choice for a given task class — e.g. as a deliberately independent review
> lane. Section 29A decides *when* to switch on failure. Section 29B decides *who
> should have been running this all along.*

> **ROUTING IS AN OPTIMIZATION LAYER, NOT A SOURCE-OF-TRUTH LAYER *(new in
> V6.5)*.** Beacon decides what the task is, what data it may expose, what
> authority it has, and what capabilities are required — the router only decides
> which *eligible* provider executes it. A provider must never be permitted to
> influence what Beacon believes, what Beacon remembers, what Beacon has decided,
> or what Beacon is authorized to execute. Concretely: swapping the provider that
> runs a task must never change the task's risk tier, its data-classification
> eligibility (Section 27A), or its authority boundary (Section 19) — if it does,
> that is a routing bug, not a routing decision.

### 29B.2 Task Classifier

A pure, rule-based function (no learned router — consistent with Phase 1.5's
existing "no learned router" constraint) that runs once per WorkUnit and reads only
fields Beacon already produces:

```
TaskClassifier(workUnit) -> taskClass

inputs (already present in existing data model):
  role                      ← fixed 8-role Phase 1.5 baseline
  riskTier                  ← existing Risk 0-3 classification
  permissionLevel           ← plan/read-only vs controlled+worktree, per role contract
  independentReviewRequired ← true whenever riskTier >= 2 (existing council-mode rule)
  contextSize               ← existing token/context telemetry
  writeRequired             ← implicit in role's permission level

output:
  taskClass ∈ {
    architecture, research, codebase_discovery, implementation,
    qa, pr_review, release, learning_explanation
  }
```

The classifier produces a label. It does not itself pick a provider — that is the
task-class table's job (29B.3), filtered through the existing routing precedence
(29B.5). This keeps the same separation of concerns Phase 1.5 already uses:
prompts describe behavior, deterministic code enforces routing.

### 29B.3 Task-class → provider table

#### 29B.3.1 Locked vs. open-ranked roles *(new in V6.4)*

Not every role should be routed the same way. Two distinct strategies apply:

- **Locked roles** — the provider set is fixed regardless of health or cost.
  Architecture is locked to Claude/Codex because the cost of an architecture
  mistake is high enough to justify spending paid capacity there specifically,
  every time. A free-tier tool is eligible only as an absolute last resort (both
  paid providers capped) and never as sole voice on riskTier 2-3 — this is
  emergency overflow, not a ranked candidate.
- **Open-ranked roles** — paid and free candidates compete on the same criteria
  (fit, cost, health — routing precedence steps 4-7, Section 29B.5), with **no
  paid-first default**. Implementation is the primary example: a simple,
  low-risk implementation task can route to a free-tier candidate even when
  Claude and Codex are both fully healthy, because the task doesn't need premium
  reasoning to do well, and paid capacity is worth conserving for where it does.

This replaces the earlier (V6.0-V6.3) structure, where every role had a single
named "Primary" that defaulted to paid, with free tier framed only as overflow.
That framing was correct for architecture; it was never the intended design for
implementation.

| Task class | Maps to role(s) | Strategy | Candidates (ranked by fit/cost/health where open) | Eligibility rule |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Architecture / ADR | chief-of-staff (routing), synthesis | **Locked** | Claude or Codex, whichever has higher benchmarked task-fit | Free tier (OpenCode / Google AI Studio) eligible **only** if both paid providers are capped, and only for riskTier ≤ 1. Never sole voice on riskTier 2-3. |
| Research | market-researcher | Open (evidence diversity) | Claude (web) and OpenCode / Google AI Studio, run **in parallel** | Free tier run alongside Claude even when Claude is available — different model family, genuine evidence diversity, not just a fallback. |
| Codebase discovery | codebase-researcher | Open, free-tier default | OpenCode / Groq, Claude (read-only) as needed | Free tier eligible by default — read-only, riskTier 0, lowest-consequence role in the system. |
| Implementation | code-writer | **Open-ranked** *(corrected in V6.4)* | Codex, Claude Code, Aider + gpt-oss-120b (dual-homed Cerebras/Groq), Aider + GLM-4.7 or Qwen3 235B — ranked by fit/cost/health, no paid-first default | Free-tier candidates eligible for riskTier 0-1 at any time, not only when paid is capped. RiskTier 2-3 implementation stays paid-only regardless of capacity. |
| QA | qa-engineer | Open, free-tier default | OpenCode / Cerebras (gpt-oss-120b or GLM-4.7), cheapest paid config as needed | Free tier eligible only for deterministic-check-only QA runs (existing rule: "cannot edit source or install deps" already makes this safe). |
| PR review | pr-reviewer | Open (independence-seeking) | Claude / Codex, provider different from implementer, or OpenCode / OpenRouter with a model family distinct from the implementer's | Free tier eligible as a genuinely **independent third lens**, not a replacement lane — extends the existing cross-provider council requirement, does not weaken it. |
| Release | release-manager | **Locked** | Claude / Codex only | **Never free-tier**, at any risk tier, at any capacity state. Matches the existing "no deploy authority for provider adapters" rule. |
| Learning explanation | Phase 1.6 Understanding Compiler output | Open, free-tier default | OpenCode / Google AI Studio + deterministic lesson template, best available paid model as needed | Lowest-stakes role; safest default candidate for free tier. |

Backend candidates (Groq, Cerebras, Google AI Studio, OpenRouter free slots) are
chosen for having **permanent**, no-credit-card free tiers backed by an
identifiable operating company, as opposed to trial-credit offers that expire or
low-provenance aggregators with no trust signal — see Section 29B.10.2 for the
vetting basis. **The specific models named above (gpt-oss-120b, GLM-4.7, Qwen3
235B) reflect what Groq and Cerebras actually host as of 2026-08-13, corrected
from the earlier, weaker Llama-3.3-70B-only assumption — see Section 29B.10.4 for
why gpt-oss-120b's dual-hosting is treated as the default pairing.**



### 29B.4 Trust tiers for third-party adapters

Any provider adapter — paid or free — must be assigned a trust tier before it is
eligible for the table above. This governs what it may touch, not whether it is
"good":

Trust tiers are assigned **separately to the harness and to the backend** — a
harness's trust level does not automatically transfer to whatever backend it is
pointed at, since the two are independently swappable (Section 29B.10):

| Tier | Harness examples | Backend examples | Allowed capability |
|---|---|---|---|
| T0 — deterministic | Tests, linters, schema validation | n/a | No LLM involved; always eligible. |
| T1 — trusted first-party | Claude Code, Codex CLI (official vendor CLI) | n/a — paid providers only | Normal repo access under existing role/permission policy. |
| T1-equivalent — vetted open source, permanent backend | OpenCode (most active, provider-agnostic), Aider (Git-native, proven) | Groq / Cerebras hosting gpt-oss-120b (dual-homed, preferred default — Section 29B.10.4), GLM-4.7, or Qwen3 235B; Google AI Studio; OpenRouter free slots — company-run, permanent free tier, no credit card required | Read-only roles by default (Section 29B.3); Aider extends to sandboxed write roles at riskTier 0-1, now open-ranked rather than fallback-only for implementation (29B.3.1). |
| T2 — approved open source, restricted | Aider on write-capable tasks | — | Sandbox/worktree only; restricted credentials; no secrets exposure. |
| T3 — experimental | OpenHands, unreviewed extensions | Trial-credit-only backends (expire in 30-90 days) | Read-only or disposable sandbox only; not in default routing table. |
| T4 — prohibited | Unvetted binaries, unknown provenance | Low-provenance aggregators with no identifiable operating company or trust signal | Never routed to, regardless of task class or capacity state. |

Vetting an adapter into T1/T2 requires: identifying the official source/maintainer,
checking license, pinning an exact version/commit, reading the tool's own
instructions and declared tool/permission surface, scanning for unexpected
network/shell behavior, and running it once in a disposable worktree against a
non-sensitive repository before it is added to the live routing table.

### 29B.5 Updated routing precedence

Extends the existing Phase 1.5 routing precedence list (Milestone 6) — nothing
below removes or reorders steps 1-2, 4-5, 7-8; steps 3 and 6 are the ones this
section fills in with concrete policy rather than leaving as unfilled criteria:

```
1. explicit user provider requirement
2. security/data-classification eligibility        ← T3/T4 tools excluded here,
                                                       before the table is consulted
3. required tool/capability availability            ← Task Classifier + trust tier
                                                       feed this step (29B.2, 29B.4)
4. provider health/capacity                          ← existing Capacity Watcher (29A)
5. independent-review requirement                    ← existing council-mode rule;
                                                       29B.3's PR-review row extends it
6. benchmarked task-fit evidence                     ← task-class table feeds this
                                                       step (29B.3) once eval data exists
7. cost/latency preference among equally qualified choices
8. configured default
```

### 29B.6 Effect on the Capacity Watcher terminal state

Section 29A's terminal state ("both paid providers capped → pause") is unchanged
as the *default*. Once 29B.3 is eval-approved for a given task class (not before —
see 29B.7), the terminal state for that task class only becomes: attempt an
eligible free-tier adapter per the table before pausing. Release and riskTier 2-3
implementation still pause on dual-cap regardless — the table's eligibility rules
override raw availability, exactly as they do when both providers are healthy.

### 29B.6.1 Free-tier failure policy, resolved *(new in V6.5, resolves 29B.9.5)*

Open decision 29B.9.5 asked whether mid-execution capacity switching should
extend to free-tier backends, or whether free-tier exhaustion should simply fail
the task back to the user. This is now resolved:

```
Eligible task
      ↓
Primary provider
      ↓
capacity warning
      ↓
same-model / compatible fallback   ← e.g. gpt-oss-120b: Cerebras → Groq (29B.10.4)
      ↓
eligible paid provider              ← only if the task class permits paid escalation
      ↓
pause
```

The dual-homing pairing (Section 29B.10.4) is the first fallback tried, precisely
because it doesn't change output shape mid-task. Only after a same-model fallback
is exhausted does the chain consider a different model or escalate to a paid
provider — and locked roles (Section 29B.3.1) never enter this chain at all:

| Role | Failure policy |
|---|---|
| Architecture | **Paid only.** No free-tier fallback at any point in the chain. |
| Release | **Paid only.** No free-tier fallback at any point in the chain. |
| RiskTier 2-3 implementation | **Paid only.** Same restriction as riskTier 2-3 everywhere else in this document. |
| Low-risk implementation / QA / learning | **Free + paid**, following the chain above, subject to the eval and trust rules already in force (29B.4, 29B.7). |

This preserves every safety boundary already established in V6.4 — it resolves
*how* the fallback chain behaves for roles where free tier was already eligible,
not *whether* locked roles ever become eligible.

### 29B.7 Honest scoping — what is not yet proven

Consistent with 29A.4's precedent and operating law 3: **as of V6.1, no free-tier
adapter has been run against a real Beacon WorkUnit, and no eval evidence exists
comparing OpenCode, Aider, or OpenHands output quality against the existing
Claude/Codex baseline on any of the eight roles.** The table in 29B.3 is a routing
*hypothesis*, not a default. Per the existing Phase 1.5 optimization acceptance
rule (no token-only optimization — a cheaper configuration is rejected if it causes
retries, missed defects, or weaker evidence), no task class may be routed to a
free-tier adapter in default operation until it passes the same eval fixture bar
Phase 1.5 already requires for paid-role configuration changes (Milestone 8).
Until then, Section 29B.2's classifier may run in **log-only mode** — recording
what it *would* route to, without controlling execution — so real comparison data
can accumulate before anything changes behavior.

**Benchmark methodology caveat (added V6.4).** Any benchmark figure cited
anywhere in this document or in future routing decisions must state which
harness produced it. As of 2026-08, SWE-bench Verified is saturated and
contamination-flagged — top frontier models cluster within 1-4 points of each
other, which is no longer a meaningful signal (Appendix C). SWE-bench Pro scores
are only comparable within the same harness: vendor-self-reported scores (each
lab's own tuned scaffold) have been observed to differ from Scale AI's
standardized SEAL harness by 10-30 points on the identical model. A benchmark
number without a stated harness should be treated as marketing, not evidence,
per operating law 6.

### 29B.8 New events (see Section 26)

`TaskClassified`, `FreeTierAdapterEligible`, `FreeTierAdapterRouted`,
`FreeTierAdapterRejected`, `AdapterTrustTierAssigned`.

### 29B.9 Open decisions (not yet resolved — do not treat as settled)

1. Which task classes get eval fixtures first — Section 32's build-order logic
   (start with lowest-risk, read-only roles) suggests codebase discovery and QA as
   the first candidates, but this is not yet decided against real backlog priority.
2. Whether Google AI Studio's Gemini backend materially improves research
   evidence diversity versus simply adding latency/noise — unmeasured.
3. Exact sandboxing mechanism for Aider (T2) write access — worktree isolation is
   assumed to carry over from existing Phase 1.5 patterns but has not been verified
   against a non-Claude/Codex write-capable adapter.
4. Source of the benchmark evidence feeding routing-precedence step 6 (29B.5) —
   external benchmark snapshots (e.g. Terminal-Bench, SWE-bench) may inform initial
   hypotheses but are not a substitute for Beacon-owned eval data, per operating
   law 6.
5. ~~Whether mid-execution capacity switching (Section 29A.3) should extend to
   free-tier backends...~~ **RESOLVED in V6.5 — see Section 29B.6.1.**
6. **(new in V6.1)** No provider or model named anywhere in this section —
   including Groq, Cerebras, Google AI Studio, and OpenRouter — should be treated
   as permanently free. Per operating law 6 and the precedent this correction
   itself sets, free-tier availability must be re-verified on some cadence, not
   assumed durable once configured.
7. **(new in V6.4)** Free-tier **model catalogs**, not just free-tier
   **availability**, need periodic re-verification — Cerebras's hosted-model list
   was observed to shrink from roughly a dozen models to two within months. The
   specific models named in Section 29B.3 (gpt-oss-120b, GLM-4.7, Qwen3 235B) are
   correct as of 2026-08-13 and should not be assumed correct indefinitely. No
   cadence for this re-verification has been decided.

### 29B.10 Harness adapter architecture *(new in V6.1)*

#### 29B.10.1 Why the harness/backend split replaced the single Gemini CLI candidate

V6.0 named Gemini CLI directly as the T1 free-tier adapter — one vendor tool
bundling the CLI harness and the model access together. Gemini CLI's free tier
ended 2026-06-18, five days after V6.0 was authored (Appendix C), which is a
direct instance of exactly the failure Section 29A.1 already warns
against: a snapshot treated as durable past its freshness window — this time in
the document itself rather than in Beacon's runtime state. The structural fix is
not "pick a different single vendor tool" (which fails the same way again later)
but to make the **harness** (the CLI process Beacon spawns) and the **backend**
(the model API that harness calls) two independently swappable components, so no
single vendor's decision can remove the whole capability at once.

#### 29B.10.2 What a harness adapter does, mechanically

A `HarnessAdapter` implements the same `ProviderAdapter` contract Claude and Codex
adapters already implement (Phase 1.5's provider-adapter boundary), but instead of
calling a vendor SDK directly, it wraps an external CLI tool as a subprocess:

```
HarnessAdapter.run(workUnit):
  1. Take the compiled prompt from the Context/Prompt Compiler
     (same stable-prefix + references artifact used for Claude/Codex)
  2. Write it into whatever input format the harness expects
     (prompt file, stdin, or CLI flags — harness-specific)
  3. Spawn the harness as a child process, with the backend credential
     (e.g. a Groq or Cerebras API key) injected as an environment variable —
     never hardcoded, so harness and backend stay independently swappable
  4. Capture stdout, stderr, and exit code
  5. Parse and normalize that output into the same AgentRun / typed-artifact
     contract Claude and Codex adapters already produce
```

Step 5 is the load-bearing step: everything downstream of the adapter layer
(QA, review, telemetry, provenance — Sections 21, 33) never needs to know whether
a Claude API call or a spawned OpenCode process produced a given artifact, because
both normalize into the identical shape.

**Token telemetry honesty for harness adapters.** Neither OpenCode nor Aider
exposes usage fields identically to Anthropic's or OpenAI's SDKs. Where the
backend API itself reports `usage` fields (most OpenAI-compatible endpoints,
including Groq, do), the harness adapter parses and normalizes those. Where it
does not, the adapter **must mark the telemetry as estimated, never silently omit
it or fabricate a precise figure** — the same rule Phase 1.5 Milestone 2 already
applies to Claude/Codex telemetry gaps.

#### 29B.10.3 Backend vetting basis

Free backends are evaluated on three criteria before entering the T1-equivalent
tier in 29B.4: (1) a **permanent** free tier, not a trial credit that expires;
(2) **no credit card required**, which correlates with lower vendor lock-in
friction; (3) an **identifiable operating company** behind it (Groq, Cerebras,
Google, or a named aggregator), as opposed to unattributed community-run
aggregators with no trust signal. This is a routing hypothesis pending eval
evidence per 29B.7, not a certified security clearance.

#### 29B.10.4 Dual-homing for free-tier backends *(new in V6.4)*

Where the **identical model weights** are served by two independent free
providers — as of 2026-08-13, gpt-oss-120b is hosted by both Cerebras and
Groq — that pairing is the preferred primary/fallback combination over two
*different* models on two different providers.

The reason is behavioral, not just availability: a same-weights failover
produces the same output format, tone, and quirks on the fallback provider,
because it's the same model. A different-model fallback changes shape exactly
when a task is already mid-execution and least able to absorb a surprise —
different formatting conventions, different refusal patterns, different failure
modes. This makes dual-homing a stronger reliability pattern than simply having
two free options available, and it is the reason gpt-oss-120b is named as the
default free-tier implementation candidate in Section 29B.3 rather than treating
Groq and Cerebras as interchangeable sources of arbitrary models.

**Honest scoping.** This is a design preference backed by the general
reliability argument above, not by a Beacon-specific incident — no Beacon
WorkUnit has yet exercised a live Cerebras→Groq (or reverse) failover. Free-tier
model *catalogs* are also independently volatile — see open decision 29B.9.7 —
so this specific pairing (gpt-oss-120b on both) must be re-verified before it is
relied on, not assumed stable because it was true on 2026-08-13.

### 29B.11 The two-stage routing decision model *(new in V6.1)*

Earlier drafts of this document collapsed provider selection into a single
"provider router" step. In practice this is two decisions, made at two different
moments, using two different mechanisms — conflating them is the easiest way to
misdescribe how switching actually works.

#### 29B.11.1 Initial routing decision (once per prompt, before execution)

```
Prompt classified (role, risk tier, task class)
  → Task-class table lookup (29B.3): which providers are eligible
  → Trust + risk filter (29B.4): hard gate, excludes ineligible tiers entirely —
    a T3 tool never enters ranking for riskTier 2-3 work, regardless of health
  → Rank remaining eligible candidates by health, latency, cost (29B.5 steps 4-7)
  → Primary provider selected, execution begins
```

The filter step is a gate, not a preference: it runs *before* ranking, so a
free-tier tool cannot win a high-stakes task merely because a paid provider was
temporarily slow. This ordering is what keeps 29B.3's per-role eligibility rules
binding rather than advisory.

#### 29B.11.2 Mid-execution switch decision (continuous, during a running task)

This is the Capacity Watcher (Section 29A.3) — a separate mechanism that does
**not** re-run the task classifier or re-rank candidates. It monitors the
already-selected provider and, on threshold crossing, hands off to whichever
candidate ranked next in the *original* ordering from 29B.11.1:

```
Execution in progress (using primary provider)
  → Capacity Watcher polls usage
  → Threshold check: warning threshold crossed → pre-generate ContinuationPackage
                       hard cap crossed        → trigger switch
  → Below threshold: continue, no action
  → At/above threshold: switch — ContinuationPackage + next eligible provider
```

**Honest scoping, restated from 29A.4:** this describes designed behavior, not
observed behavior. No real capacity-cap event has triggered this mechanism in
production as of V6.1. Section 29B.9 item 5 also leaves open whether this
mid-execution mechanism extends to free-tier backends at all, or whether
free-tier exhaustion should simply fail the task back to the user.

---

## 30. Tool stack and infrastructure restraint

| Need | Initial choice | Deferred until evidence |
|---|---|---|
| Canonical prose/metadata | Existing Markdoc/content model | New editor/database only if constraints emerge. |
| Architecture/process/state/ERD | Mermaid | Specialized graph canvas only when interaction requirements justify it. |
| Concept maps | Markmap | Alternative engine only if Markmap can't satisfy accessibility/interaction. |
| Exploratory drawing | Excalidraw | Collaborative whiteboard infrastructure later if genuinely required. |
| Matrices/timelines/readiness | Beacon-native Astro components | Component library expansion only when repeated patterns justify it. |
| Knowledge relations | Typed relations + generated JSON indexes | Graph database after measured traversal/query need. |
| Retrieval | Deterministic indexes + search | Embeddings/full RAG after measured retrieval gaps. |
| Rich graph interaction | None initially | React Flow only with evidence + ADR. |
| Block/editor runtime | None initially | BlockSuite only with evidence + ADR. |
| **Provider capacity monitoring** *(new)* | **Poll existing provider-exposed signals only** | **No dedicated monitoring service until Section 29A.6 item 1 is resolved with real evidence.** |
| **Task classification / routing** *(new in V6)* | **Log-only classifier (Section 29B.2); no execution control** | **Live routing control per task class only after that class passes the Milestone 8 eval bar — see Section 29B.7.** |
| **Free-tier provider adapters** *(V6.1, corrected)* | **None deployed; HarnessAdapter (OpenCode) + Groq/Cerebras/Google AI Studio backend first candidate — see Section 29B.10** | **AiderAdapter and OpenHandsAdapter (T3) after the OpenCode-based HarnessAdapter has real eval evidence; OpenHands stays out of the default routing table indefinitely per Section 29B.4.** |

