  closed-source Antigravity CLI, whose free tier dropped from roughly 1,000
  requests/day to roughly 20. Direct cause of Addendum V3's harness/backend
  correction (Section 29B.10). Cited as the precedent for open decision 29B.9
  item 6: no free-tier provider in this document should be assumed permanent.**
  *(new reference in V6.1)*
- Free-tier LLM API landscape review, sourced 2026-08-13 (OpenRouter, Groq,
  Cerebras, and independent trade-press comparisons, cross-checked against
  provider-published rate-limit documentation) — **basis for the backend vetting
  criteria in Section 29B.10.3 (permanent free tier, no credit card, identifiable
  operating company). Point-in-time third-party data, not Beacon-owned eval
  evidence, per operating law 6.** *(new reference in V6.1)*
- Explorable explanations and AI-generated interactive content evaluation
  literature review, sourced 2026-08-13 (Victor 2011; CHI 2026 Transformer
  Explainer; EE-Eval, AIED 2026) — **basis for Section 17A's explorable-first
  pipeline and Section 17B's evaluation checkpoint. Confirms the underlying
  learning-science citations already in this document (Appendix B [R1]-[R13])
  have a corresponding engineering literature for AI-generated interactive
  content specifically, not just human-authored material.** *(new reference in
  V6.2)*
- Architecture decision record supersession and versioning practice review,
  sourced 2026-08-13 (AWS Architecture Blog; TechTarget; MADR project; multiple
  independent team retrospectives) — **basis for Section 19A's supersession
  contract. Cross-source consensus: accepted ADRs are immutable, superseding
  decisions are new documents with bidirectional links, and unenforced status
  fields silently rot without a governance mechanism — the last point is the
  specific justification for making `supersedesAdrRef`/`supersededByAdrRef`
  machine-checked fields rather than prose convention.** *(new reference in
  V6.2)*
- SWE-bench Verified/Pro and Terminal-Bench 2.1 leaderboard review, sourced
  2026-08-13 (BenchLM.ai, llm-stats vendor aggregate, Scale AI SEAL standardized
  leaderboard, morphllm.com) — **basis for the benchmark methodology caveat in
  Section 29B.7. Confirms SWE-bench Verified is saturated and
  contamination-flagged (OpenAI stopped reporting it in February 2026) and that
  vendor-self-reported SWE-bench Pro scores can differ from Scale's standardized
  SEAL harness by 10-30 points on the identical model — e.g. Opus 4.8 reported at
  69.2% (vendor aggregate) versus 51.9% (Scale standardized).** *(new reference
  in V6.4)*
- Free-tier model catalog review, sourced 2026-08-13 (ianlpaterson.com live-probe
  of Groq/Cerebras/Mistral catalogs; wotai.co ~110-model provider directory;
  buildfastwithai.com open-weight model comparison) — **basis for the corrected
  free-tier model recommendations in Section 29B.3 (gpt-oss-120b, GLM-4.7, Qwen3
  235B, replacing the earlier Llama-3.3-70B-only assumption) and the dual-homing
  pattern in Section 29B.10.4. Directly evidences open decision 29B.9.7: one
  source's live probe found Cerebras's hosted-model list shrink from roughly a
  dozen models to two within months.** *(new reference in V6.4)*
- Claude Code usage-signal review, sourced 2026-08-14 (morphllm.com;
  voitanos.io status-line documentation; a GitHub gist documenting the
  `GET /api/oauth/usage` endpoint's response shape directly) — **partially
  resolves open decision 29A.6 item 1. Confirms Claude Code's status line and
  `/usage` command surface `five_hour.utilization` and `seven_day.utilization`
  as structured percentages with `resets_at` timestamps, cross-confirmed across
  independent sources including a direct endpoint response sample. Does not
  independently confirm the equivalent for Codex — that half of the open
  decision remains unresolved. Basis for Section 29A.7.** *(new reference in
  V6.5.1)*

